1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,380
Over 200 tons, they blast off every two minutes, cruising at close to the speed of sound.

2
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:12,820
They are among the most complex and powerful systems on Earth, yet we take them for granted.

3
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,100
Now, commercial jets on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:17,101 --> 00:00:30,680
Everett, Washington

5
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:33,620
In the spring of 1967

6
00:00:33,621 --> 00:00:36,680
it was 700 acres of rock and timber

7
00:00:36,681 --> 00:00:40,000
In a little more than six months

8
00:00:40,001 --> 00:00:42,940
it would be transformed into a high-tech city

9
00:00:42,941 --> 00:00:46,240
populated by an army of highly skilled men and women

10
00:00:46,241 --> 00:00:48,740
that would eventually total 20,000.

11
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:51,580
Commissioned by the Boeing Aircraft Company,

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,740
their mission was simply to build the biggest and fastest

13
00:00:54,741 --> 00:00:57,520
subsonic commercial jetliner ever constructed.

14
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:03,260
It would carry over 350 passengers at over 600 miles an hour.

15
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:06,000
Its name was 747.

16
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:12,480
747 really was a quantum leap in capacity and capabilities

17
00:01:12,481 --> 00:01:18,980
is that if you were planning the growth of an industry in a logical, progressive way,

18
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:20,060
you probably wouldn't do that.

19
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:26,640
You probably wouldn't grow an airplane 150% larger than the one that came before.

20
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:34,760
Boeing President Bill Allen assigned Vice President Mal Stamper to head the 747 project.

21
00:01:36,860 --> 00:01:41,580
Allen said, how would you like to build the biggest airplane in the world?

22
00:01:41,581 --> 00:01:47,820
And so Stamper, he leaped to it, you know, and moved his bed up there.

23
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:56,100
The 747 project demanded the design and construction of 270,000 tools.

24
00:01:56,780 --> 00:02:01,960
But the first tools that went to work on the original jumbo jet were not welding rigs and rivet guns.

25
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:04,620
They were chainsaws and bulldozers.

26
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,720
So big was the project that no building existed that could house it.

27
00:02:09,721 --> 00:02:16,760
one would have to be built 780 acres of forest were cleared 45 miles north of

28
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:17,461
Seattle

29
00:02:17,462 --> 00:02:23,160
next to an abandoned World War two airfield a two-mile spur of railroad track

30
00:02:23,161 --> 00:02:27,240
had to be added to the main rail to move building materials up to the plant site

31
00:02:27,241 --> 00:02:32,400
34,000 tons of steel arrived by rail

32
00:02:32,401 --> 00:02:36,560
for the initial construction of the 200 million cubic foot structure

33
00:02:36,561 --> 00:02:39,560
It would become the biggest building in the world.

34
00:02:41,340 --> 00:02:45,440
Later, the same track would be used to bring in the gigantic sub-assemblies

35
00:02:45,441 --> 00:02:51,400
from 20,000 contractors in all 50 United States and eight countries around the world.

36
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:58,180
The total assembly would require over 4 million individually numbered and catalogued parts.

37
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,560
The project would take 5 years, 5 billion dollars.

38
00:03:02,561 --> 00:03:06,060
It would consume 10 million engineering man-hours.

39
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:11,280
and fourteen thousand hours of wind tunnel testing.

40
00:03:11,281 --> 00:03:14,520
The first designs dated from 1965

41
00:03:14,521 --> 00:03:18,780
when Pan American Airlines president Juan Tripp bankrolled the project with a

42
00:03:18,781 --> 00:03:21,160
quarter-billion dollar down payment

43
00:03:21,161 --> 00:03:24,760
for twenty-five of the monster planes.

44
00:03:24,761 --> 00:03:29,780
By the spring of 1967, the giant sub-assemblies began to arrive

45
00:03:29,781 --> 00:03:33,000
while plant construction was still underway.

46
00:03:33,001 --> 00:03:35,980
Boeing employees called it the Aluminum Avalanche.

47
00:03:36,860 --> 00:03:40,060
Stamper's rugged 747 team was also given a nickname.

48
00:03:41,060 --> 00:03:42,840
Stamper called them the Incredibles.

49
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:48,200
They wore Paul Bunyan stickers on their hard hats and their lunch pails,

50
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:49,860
and they wouldn't go home at the end of the shift.

51
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:57,160
Working around the clock, the Incredibles struggled to meet the 1969 deadline,

52
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:00,640
when the first 747 was due to roll off the line.

53
00:04:00,641 --> 00:04:08,320
And it all comes down to a certain point on the factory floor where the first center section

54
00:04:08,321 --> 00:04:13,340
goes in and then all the parts begin to flow into it.

55
00:04:13,341 --> 00:04:16,760
That flow began with a massive wings.

56
00:04:16,761 --> 00:04:21,540
After being assembled in huge fixtures called jigs, they were joined to the enormous center

57
00:04:21,541 --> 00:04:24,520
section made of titanium.

58
00:04:24,521 --> 00:04:31,820
Each wing with its 37 degrees of sweep measured 120 feet in length, 26 feet in width and was

59
00:04:31,821 --> 00:04:34,480
7 feet thick at its root.

60
00:04:34,481 --> 00:04:38,500
52,000 rivets went into the assembly of each wing.

61
00:04:38,501 --> 00:04:43,840
The overwing section of the fuselage was then fitted to complete the center section.

62
00:04:43,841 --> 00:04:49,580
The brute lifting in the plant was performed by 26 overhead cranes that traveled on 39

63
00:04:49,581 --> 00:04:51,840
miles of network track.

64
00:04:51,841 --> 00:04:56,480
The combined lifting capacity was 1.9 million pounds.

65
00:04:56,481 --> 00:05:02,780
Next, the nose and forward body sections were lifted into place and joined to the center section.

66
00:05:02,781 --> 00:05:11,840
The 747 was the first design with the cockpit up above the deck, 29 feet above the runway.

67
00:05:11,841 --> 00:05:16,400
The aft body section followed, complete with galleys and lavatories.

68
00:05:16,401 --> 00:05:22,720
The jet's cavernous airframe was completed by the tail cone, horizontal stabilizer, and vertical fin.

69
00:05:23,420 --> 00:05:28,020
The tail assembly stood 63 feet above the deck, the height of a six-story building.

70
00:05:29,460 --> 00:05:33,860
Once all fuselage sections were bolted and riveted into one gigantic shell,

71
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:39,180
the landing gears were installed to allow the Big Bird to be moved down the line on its own feet.

72
00:05:40,840 --> 00:05:45,420
The landing gear was comprised of four separate sections of four wheels each,

73
00:05:45,421 --> 00:05:48,180
plus a nose gear with two additional wheels.

74
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:51,980
Cargo holds, seats, wall and ceiling panels,

75
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:55,660
and in-cabin entertainment equipment were added at this stage.

76
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:59,920
The final items included radar antennas and landing gear doors.

77
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:04,220
As the project proceeded, a major problem became obvious.

78
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:07,020
The 747 had a weight problem.

79
00:06:07,980 --> 00:06:12,160
Various modifications to the original plans were adding weight at an alarming rate.

80
00:06:12,161 --> 00:06:18,680
The original 550,000 pound design weight had jumped to 710,000 pounds.

81
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:24,620
The Incredibles fought back, using composite materials and titanium instead of heavier materials.

82
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:30,640
The 8,500 pounds of titanium alone yielded a weight savings of over a ton.

83
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:34,720
The diet worked, and the project continued.

84
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:40,980
The final step was the installation of the Pratt & Whitney JT9D3 engines,

85
00:06:40,981 --> 00:06:45,440
each capable of producing 43,500 pounds of thrust.

86
00:06:47,060 --> 00:06:51,200
Once all components were installed, they were tested and retested.

87
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:55,760
The fuselage was pressurized, and all pressure seals were tested again.

88
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:00,900
Once the testing phase was completed, the giant bird was towed to the paint hanger,

89
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:06,580
where 300 gallons of paint added another 1,200 pounds to its maximum empty weight.

90
00:07:06,581 --> 00:07:12,440
Finally, the customer's insignia, or livery, were added to the plane's exterior.

91
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,300
By 1969, all the questions had been answered except one.

92
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:18,720
Would it fly?

93
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:26,840
At 11.34 on February 9, 1969, that question was answered with an unqualified yes.

94
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:34,300
After the usual debugging process inherent in any new plane, the 747 went into commercial

95
00:07:34,301 --> 00:07:39,620
service on January 22, 1970.

96
00:07:39,621 --> 00:07:47,920
Since the first prototype rolled out in 1969, more than 1,300 747s have been produced.

97
00:07:47,921 --> 00:07:55,020
Three decades later, the 747 is still the biggest and fastest subsonic jet in the air.

98
00:07:55,021 --> 00:08:01,240
Since its entry into commercial service in 1970, approximately 1.6 billion passengers

99
00:08:01,241 --> 00:08:03,900
have flown on a 747.

100
00:08:03,901 --> 00:08:10,900
The 747 fleet has logged 20 billion miles, the equivalent of 42,000 trips to the moon

101
00:08:10,901 --> 00:08:12,900
and back.

102
00:08:12,901 --> 00:08:20,700
Next, the pioneers of jet power, the engineer and the test pilot.

103
00:08:20,701 --> 00:08:26,340
The first manned flight by Orville Wright in 1903 covered a distance of 120 feet.

104
00:08:26,341 --> 00:08:30,100
This equals the length of one 747 wing.

105
00:08:30,101 --> 00:08:34,940
Commercial jets will return on Modern Marvels.

106
00:08:34,941 --> 00:08:40,480
The Wright brothers first manned flight in December of 1903 ushered in the era of powering

107
00:08:40,481 --> 00:08:45,620
fixed wing aircraft with internal combustion piston engines.

108
00:08:45,621 --> 00:08:50,520
For almost two decades aircraft engine builders accepted the conventional wisdom that any

109
00:08:50,521 --> 00:08:54,814
increase in power would come from more efficient piston engines.

110
00:08:54,815 --> 00:08:56,760
But by the 40s, every advance

111
00:08:56,761 --> 00:09:01,020
in power brought unwanted increases in weight, fuel storage and maintenance.

112
00:09:01,021 --> 00:09:08,760
During the war, at about 1942 or 1943, piston engines had just about reached their maximum

113
00:09:08,761 --> 00:09:15,078
size. The United States was developing 3,500 horsepower piston engines.

114
00:09:15,079 --> 00:09:16,680
The engines became

115
00:09:16,681 --> 00:09:22,360
so complex that they are become monstrosities heavier and in the yield an additional horsepower

116
00:09:22,361 --> 00:09:29,240
was was relatively slight when you have a spinning propeller it is in fact an airfoil

117
00:09:30,120 --> 00:09:35,080
it's spinning around it is approaching the speed of sound a lot sooner than the wing will

118
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:42,280
when that happens you get lots of vibration you get high drag and basically it's a speed limiting

119
00:09:42,281 --> 00:09:42,981
event.

120
00:09:42,982 --> 00:09:49,280
Taking a propeller out of the equation means that you're basically taking the cap

121
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:54,300
off of the speed, at least as far as engine dynamics are concerned.

122
00:09:54,301 --> 00:09:59,240
During this time in England and in Germany, two men had been working independently on

123
00:09:59,241 --> 00:10:02,431
a technology that would revolutionize air travel.

124
00:10:02,432 --> 00:10:04,840
The German was a young engineer named

125
00:10:04,841 --> 00:10:09,692
Hans von Oheim. The Englishman was RAF officer Frank Whittle.

126
00:10:09,693 --> 00:10:11,840
The technology was aircraft

127
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:17,680
jet propulsion. The concept of jet propulsion was nothing new. The challenge

128
00:10:17,681 --> 00:10:21,520
lay in using it to power airplanes.

129
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:27,440
The most common form of jet engine today is the turbojet. It uses spinning

130
00:10:27,441 --> 00:10:32,340
compressor wheels at the front end of the engine to compress the incoming air. The

131
00:10:32,341 --> 00:10:36,820
air is then mixed with fuel and ignited and the blast of exhaust gases out the

132
00:10:36,821 --> 00:10:41,380
back propels the engine forward as well as rotating turbine wheels at the back

133
00:10:41,381 --> 00:10:44,380
which turned the compression wheels on the shaft.

134
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:51,380
Whittle struggled for years to get the British government to take his jet engine concept seriously.

135
00:10:51,381 --> 00:10:53,780
Frank Whittle was a wonderful man.

136
00:10:53,781 --> 00:10:57,380
He was irascible often and somewhat short-tempered.

137
00:10:57,381 --> 00:11:02,380
He had come forward with the idea of the jet engine in the early 1930s

138
00:11:02,381 --> 00:11:06,380
and had been ignored by his government, given really, not ridiculed,

139
00:11:06,381 --> 00:11:09,380
but really not given the attention that it should have been.

140
00:11:09,381 --> 00:11:16,380
Meanwhile, von Ohain's work drew the immediate interest and support of a leading German aircraft builder, Ernst Heinkel.

141
00:11:16,381 --> 00:11:22,380
In 1936, Heinkel financed von Ohain's development of a jet engine prototype.

142
00:11:22,381 --> 00:11:29,380
By 1939, von Ohain's engine was being married to a swept-wing fighter airframe.

143
00:11:29,381 --> 00:11:34,380
And in August of that year, just one month before the Nazi invasion of Poland,

144
00:11:34,381 --> 00:11:41,380
to Poland, the HE-178 became the first jet-powered aircraft to fly.

145
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:46,730
In England, Whittle's work was finally getting serious backing from the government.

146
00:11:46,731 --> 00:11:47,431
This new

147
00:11:47,432 --> 00:11:52,260
focus was brought to the attention of a visiting American Air Force general.

148
00:11:52,261 --> 00:11:58,359
In March of 1941, General Henry Hap Arnold was on a fact-finding visit to England.

149
00:11:58,360 --> 00:11:59,060
America

150
00:11:59,061 --> 00:12:04,340
was not yet in the war, but the U.S. was looking for ways it might help the British.

151
00:12:04,341 --> 00:12:09,740
Upon seeing Whittle's engine, Arnold saw an opportunity to help England and get his country

152
00:12:09,741 --> 00:12:12,189
into the jet age with one giant leap.

153
00:12:12,190 --> 00:12:16,160
He immediately made arrangements with the British government

154
00:12:16,161 --> 00:12:20,960
to have Whittle's W-1 engine and research shipped to General Electric Corporation at

155
00:12:20,961 --> 00:12:24,098
Lynn, Massachusetts. His purpose was to allow U.S.

156
00:12:24,099 --> 00:12:26,420
aircraft builders to study it as part

157
00:12:26,421 --> 00:12:27,431
of the war effort.

158
00:12:27,432 --> 00:12:31,140
Boeing and several other airframe makers were invited to study the

159
00:12:31,141 --> 00:12:37,060
W-1 and submit plans to incorporate the new engine into a viable high-speed, long-range

160
00:12:37,061 --> 00:12:38,285
military aircraft.

161
00:12:38,286 --> 00:12:42,980
Six years and many design hours later, Boeing parlayed this knowledge

162
00:12:42,981 --> 00:12:44,355
into the B-47 bomber.

163
00:12:44,356 --> 00:12:48,740
The B-47 was really the most important multi-jet bomber in history.

164
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:56,060
From the B-47 came not only the 707, the KC-135, and the B-52, but all the Boeing designs.

165
00:12:56,061 --> 00:13:03,200
Whittle's engine design was not the only technology that made the B-47 such a revolutionary aircraft.

166
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:10,480
Boeing's access to German swept wing technology, captured in the final years of the war, was the second part of the coup.

167
00:13:11,480 --> 00:13:13,280
Boeing's wind tunnel was the third.

168
00:13:13,940 --> 00:13:17,140
Boeing got started with the B-47, as I mentioned before.

169
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:21,760
It was a $13 million project, and it was probably the best $13 million anybody ever invested.

170
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:24,600
But the B-47 had a 35-degree swept wing.

171
00:13:24,601 --> 00:13:31,000
After thousands of wind tunnel tests, Boeing learned that as a straight wing approaches supersonic speed,

172
00:13:31,720 --> 00:13:35,740
a layer of air builds up along its leading edge, increasing drag.

173
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:41,900
The leading edge of an angled or swept wing is able to slice through the air at high speed,

174
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:44,700
delaying the buildup of this unwanted layer.

175
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:51,840
The U.S. had beaten England to the punch in the production of large military jets.

176
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:58,800
In the years after the war, the British were determined to be the first in the air with a jet-powered commercial aircraft.

177
00:13:59,540 --> 00:14:06,060
At the time, America's Douglas Aircraft Company ruled the commercial skies with their piston-powered DC-3s,

178
00:14:06,400 --> 00:14:08,640
which carried 90% of the world's air traffic.

179
00:14:09,200 --> 00:14:13,940
The Americans seemed content to continue making jets for military use only.

180
00:14:13,941 --> 00:14:20,820
In 1949, Britain's de Havilland aircraft company stunned the aviation world with the

181
00:14:20,821 --> 00:14:28,080
unveiling of a sleek 93-foot-long jetliner prototype with 20-degree swept wings and two

182
00:14:28,081 --> 00:14:31,160
Whittle-designed jet engines buried in each wing route.

183
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:36,160
It was christened the de Havilland Comet.

184
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:42,700
It could carry 36 passengers and a crew of six at a cruising speed of almost 500 miles

185
00:14:42,701 --> 00:14:48,151
an hour, 50% faster than the fastest piston engine plane.

186
00:14:48,152 --> 00:14:51,020
Three years later in 1952, the

187
00:14:51,021 --> 00:14:54,521
first comet entered commercial service.

188
00:14:54,522 --> 00:14:59,100
But within two years, two comets had crashed. After

189
00:14:59,101 --> 00:15:05,080
exhaustive investigations, it was determined the comets crashed because of explosive decompression.

190
00:15:05,640 --> 00:15:10,980
The comet had square windows, which seems innocuous enough when you think about it,

191
00:15:10,981 --> 00:15:17,240
except that a square is a point at which a fatigue failure can start more easily than a round window.

192
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:19,760
And in fact, that's what happened with the comets.

193
00:15:20,100 --> 00:15:25,140
Very, very tiny cracks occurred in the corner of the windows.

194
00:15:25,660 --> 00:15:29,880
And once they occurred, it was just like ripping open a gigantic bag.

195
00:15:29,881 --> 00:15:32,820
It just blew apart because the inside differential pressure was so great.

196
00:15:33,440 --> 00:15:39,440
In the years it would take to recover, Britain's lead would be taken by the Americans and never regained.

197
00:15:40,980 --> 00:15:44,920
Next, the Americans build the first truly successful jetliner.

198
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:52,060
At any given moment, there are approximately 40,000 commercial jet passengers in the skies over North America.

199
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:55,800
Commercial jets will return on Modern Marvels.

200
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:03,020
The post-war prosperity of the 1950s brought increases in personal income.

201
00:16:04,220 --> 00:16:09,040
Consumers were able to afford the things they wanted, and one of the things they wanted was travel.

202
00:16:09,041 --> 00:16:15,780
In 1951, Boeing President William Allen traveled to England with his top engineers

203
00:16:15,781 --> 00:16:18,900
to witness one of the first test flights of the comet.

204
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:25,080
He was impressed with a basic concept, but felt the scale of the plane was all wrong.

205
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:31,260
So when Boeing decided, saw the comet, and decided that they had better get into business,

206
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:35,740
the big consensus at Boeing was to advise their president, Bill Allen,

207
00:16:35,741 --> 00:16:38,560
that they could build not only a jet airplane,

208
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:40,260
but they'd build one better than the Comet.

209
00:16:42,120 --> 00:16:44,000
Allen knew the jets were the future.

210
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:45,820
Still, he was reluctant.

211
00:16:46,480 --> 00:16:49,480
All previous Boeing commercial transports had been unsuccessful.

212
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:52,020
They lost money on the 377.

213
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:53,680
They lost money on the 247.

214
00:16:53,681 --> 00:16:55,320
They were simply not in the business.

215
00:16:55,740 --> 00:16:58,480
Douglas, on the other hand, was in the transport business.

216
00:16:58,940 --> 00:17:00,140
The airlines loved them.

217
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:02,160
They had a wonderful relationship with them.

218
00:17:02,161 --> 00:17:06,220
Everybody flew DC-3s and DC-4s and DC-6s and DC-7s.

219
00:17:06,740 --> 00:17:12,620
In Boeing's favor was the experience it had gained on producing the jet bombers B-47 and B-52.

220
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:17,460
But Alan knew his company couldn't survive on military contracts alone.

221
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:26,180
On April 22, 1952, Alan rolled the dice and sank $15 million,

222
00:17:26,860 --> 00:17:29,160
a quarter of the corporation's net assets,

223
00:17:29,161 --> 00:17:32,140
and to the development of a new jet transport.

224
00:17:32,980 --> 00:17:38,860
It began life as the 386-80, but would eventually be renamed 707.

225
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:41,660
Failure would mean bankruptcy.

226
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:46,060
Success could mean riches and a giant lead in a new market.

227
00:17:47,540 --> 00:17:49,740
Learning from mistakes made by de Havilland,

228
00:17:50,420 --> 00:17:53,180
Boeing knew that the fuselage would have to be able to withstand

229
00:17:53,181 --> 00:17:55,320
thousands of cycles of pressurization.

230
00:17:56,060 --> 00:18:04,000
What Boeing did was they built a big tank, water tank, 100 feet long, 20 feet wide and 20 feet deep.

231
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:06,360
And they built an entire fuselage.

232
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:14,780
And they put this fuselage in the tank and they pumped water in and out over a period to simulate cycles of flight.

233
00:18:14,781 --> 00:18:26,100
So based on that information, a whole new set of design criteria was developed for long-life pressurized airplanes.

234
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:27,840
That's where it all started.

235
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:31,540
Going back to what they had learned on their large bomber projects,

236
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:37,080
Boeing engineers designed the airliner with swept-back wings angled at 35 degrees.

237
00:18:38,040 --> 00:18:42,960
Instead of placing the wings at the top of the fuselage, they created a low-wing design.

238
00:18:44,780 --> 00:18:51,460
The 707 was designed to carry 130 passengers, more than three times as many as the Comet's 36.

239
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:58,220
Its four turbojet engines were pod-mounted out on the wings rather than buried next to the fuselage.

240
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,560
This design would prevent an engine fire from spreading into the cabin.

241
00:19:03,960 --> 00:19:08,200
The cruising speed was 535 miles per hour.

242
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:13,520
The airframe was subjected to 4,240 hours of wind tunnel testing.

243
00:19:14,780 --> 00:19:21,780
The fuselage was reinforced with titanium tear stoppers to guard against stress cracks.

244
00:19:21,781 --> 00:19:26,780
In other words, if you're going to get a crack, you don't want it to run and become catastrophic.

245
00:19:26,781 --> 00:19:32,780
You want it to stop. So they built what they called crack stoppers into the design.

246
00:19:32,781 --> 00:19:37,780
The first 707 was air tested on July 15, 1954.

247
00:19:37,781 --> 00:19:40,780
It passed with high praise from the pilots.

248
00:19:40,781 --> 00:19:41,947
In 1955, the U.S.

249
00:19:41,948 --> 00:19:47,780
Air Force ordered 29 of the planes, but still there were no orders from the airlines.

250
00:19:47,781 --> 00:19:51,780
Finally, a few orders trickled in.

251
00:19:51,781 --> 00:19:56,780
Then suddenly, out of nowhere, Douglas Aircraft Company delivered a startling announcement.

252
00:19:56,781 --> 00:20:00,780
They would build a similar jet called the DC-8.

253
00:20:00,781 --> 00:20:05,780
The crafty Donald Douglas had waited for Boeing to commit to the 707 design.

254
00:20:05,781 --> 00:20:12,780
He announced to the airlines that the DC-8 would be larger than the 707, with six abreast seating rather than five.

255
00:20:12,781 --> 00:20:18,780
The DC-8 was nicknamed the paper airplane, since it was sold while still on the drawing board.

256
00:20:18,781 --> 00:20:23,780
And the interesting thing was that such was the airline's love of Douglas,

257
00:20:23,781 --> 00:20:29,780
and such was their confidence in Douglas, that they were willing to buy DC-8s before they'd even got off the drawing board almost,

258
00:20:29,781 --> 00:20:32,780
even though they had a 707 flying.

259
00:20:32,781 --> 00:20:39,460
Boeing's Bill Allen had too much invested to let Douglas Trump him.

260
00:20:39,461 --> 00:20:44,860
While continuing to sell his original 707s, he ordered his engineers to widen the fuselage

261
00:20:44,861 --> 00:20:50,740
by 16 inches and install more powerful Pratt & Whitney JT-4 engines.

262
00:20:50,741 --> 00:20:55,780
This second version of the 707 was called the Intercontinental, and it truly was.

263
00:20:55,781 --> 00:20:59,420
It was the first true long-range commercial jet.

264
00:20:59,421 --> 00:21:02,960
The airlines and the flying public loved it.

265
00:21:02,961 --> 00:21:07,460
People got on board the airplane, could not believe how quiet it was, how smooth it was.

266
00:21:07,461 --> 00:21:09,440
I mean, they were flying above the weather, you know.

267
00:21:09,441 --> 00:21:13,820
There had never been previously in commercial air transportation a similar forward leap.

268
00:21:13,821 --> 00:21:19,440
This was an entirely new notch that Boeing had achieved.

269
00:21:19,441 --> 00:21:23,980
Over at Douglas, the company rushed its paper airplane into production, knowing they had

270
00:21:23,981 --> 00:21:26,260
to get it right the first time.

271
00:21:26,261 --> 00:21:30,260
The grueling production timetable almost wrecked the company.

272
00:21:30,261 --> 00:21:33,004
Unfortunately for Douglas, they didn't have the management.

273
00:21:33,005 --> 00:21:34,260
They were an older company,

274
00:21:34,261 --> 00:21:39,260
and they had run into management and production difficulties, so the DC-8 really overstressed them.

275
00:21:41,260 --> 00:21:47,260
Once the long-range transcontinental market had been conquered by 707s and DC-8s,

276
00:21:47,261 --> 00:21:52,260
the airlines began to ask for short-range jets that could provide economical jet service

277
00:21:52,261 --> 00:21:55,260
between major cities in Europe and the U.S.

278
00:21:55,261 --> 00:21:59,640
Jets that could service airports with runways too small for the 707s.

279
00:22:01,300 --> 00:22:08,760
Well, the next major step, I would say, is that at that time, small jets weren't economical.

280
00:22:09,980 --> 00:22:16,720
The challenge to the industry was to build an airplane, a jet airplane, that was smaller.

281
00:22:17,560 --> 00:22:22,880
European plane makers tried to fill this market with several designs that carried less than 100 seats.

282
00:22:22,881 --> 00:22:29,320
But it soon became obvious that larger payloads and more power were needed, especially in America.

283
00:22:30,200 --> 00:22:34,980
United insisted that the airplane operate out of Denver with good results.

284
00:22:35,380 --> 00:22:39,740
United has Denver as a hub, and Denver is a mile high.

285
00:22:40,480 --> 00:22:48,140
So the criteria for taking off and landing an airplane at Denver is much different than at sea level like Seattle or Los Angeles.

286
00:22:48,141 --> 00:22:55,140
Denver's thin atmosphere required a plane with extra power at takeoff.

287
00:22:55,141 --> 00:23:02,140
Airports with short runways, like LaGuardia, demanded a jet capable of steep, quick descents.

288
00:23:02,141 --> 00:23:07,140
The airlines wanted one plane that could handle both.

289
00:23:07,141 --> 00:23:10,140
Now, each airline had a particular requirement.

290
00:23:10,141 --> 00:23:16,140
Now, United wanted a shorter-haul airplane to operate more economically over shorter-range distance,

291
00:23:16,141 --> 00:23:19,140
a quarter range distance, and Eastern wanted it.

292
00:23:19,141 --> 00:23:21,140
United wanted a four-engine airplane,

293
00:23:21,141 --> 00:23:23,140
Eastern wanted a two-engine airplane.

294
00:23:23,141 --> 00:23:25,140
So Boeing had to come up with some kind of a compromise,

295
00:23:25,141 --> 00:23:27,140
and the really deciding, a tough factor,

296
00:23:27,141 --> 00:23:31,140
was that Eastern's airplanes had to lift off a 4,860-foot runway

297
00:23:31,141 --> 00:23:35,140
at LaGuardia and fly to Miami nonstop.

298
00:23:35,141 --> 00:23:41,140
Boeing's team of 1,000 engineers looked at over 150 design studies

299
00:23:41,141 --> 00:23:45,140
and evaluated the airline's requirements.

300
00:23:45,141 --> 00:23:50,080
On December 1st of 1960, Boeing's President William Allen stepped up to the table once

301
00:23:50,081 --> 00:23:53,660
again and bet the ranch on the new design.

302
00:23:53,661 --> 00:23:56,480
It would be called 727.

303
00:23:56,481 --> 00:24:02,060
It would use the same fuselage width as the 707, providing six abreast seating for 120

304
00:24:02,061 --> 00:24:03,460
passengers.

305
00:24:03,461 --> 00:24:09,320
It was powered by three of Pratt & Whitney's new JT8D turbofan engines, which produced

306
00:24:09,321 --> 00:24:12,640
15,000 pounds of thrust each.

307
00:24:12,641 --> 00:24:18,580
Together, they could easily launch a loaded 727 into Denver's thin, mile-high skies.

308
00:24:19,400 --> 00:24:23,060
Three-engine jet instead of four-engine jet, there are a lot of obvious advantages.

309
00:24:23,340 --> 00:24:27,260
You cut down on your inventory costs right away with a three-engine jet.

310
00:24:27,620 --> 00:24:30,900
You cut down on your fuel costs with a three-engine jet compared to a four-engine jet.

311
00:24:31,720 --> 00:24:36,280
But the technology that put 727 in a class by itself was its wing.

312
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:41,120
The 727 wing was sleek and aerodynamic at altitude for speed.

313
00:24:41,121 --> 00:24:46,120
And, thanks to a unique design of multi-layered retractable flaps,

314
00:24:46,121 --> 00:24:51,120
it could expand its surface by 25% when landing and taking off.

315
00:24:51,121 --> 00:24:56,120
This greatly increased the plane's ability to navigate short runways.

316
00:24:58,120 --> 00:25:02,120
This was the first time in industrial history where they had to split the flap.

317
00:25:02,121 --> 00:25:06,120
And it turned out that this was exactly what the doctor ordered.

318
00:25:06,121 --> 00:25:10,120
It's like when you see a bird land and the feathers all come out.

319
00:25:10,121 --> 00:25:13,020
Well, there's a lot of feathers that come out on the 727.

320
00:25:14,060 --> 00:25:19,000
When the first 727 rolled out on November 27, 1962,

321
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:24,880
Boeing engineers discovered that it was 15 knots faster than the wind tunnel tests had predicted.

322
00:25:25,440 --> 00:25:30,380
It consumed 10% less fuel and could carry greater payloads than anticipated.

323
00:25:32,200 --> 00:25:38,320
When the 727 went into commercial service, it rapidly developed a reputation as a pilot's plane.

324
00:25:39,200 --> 00:25:45,140
Its responsive handling characteristics were praised as being more like those of a small fighter than a huge airliner.

325
00:25:45,880 --> 00:25:49,320
But this reputation would prove a source of trouble for the new jet.

326
00:25:50,820 --> 00:25:56,340
After 19 months in service, 727s began to experience approach crashes.

327
00:25:56,720 --> 00:26:02,580
With four accidents in eight months, the ghost of the comet began to stalk the halls of Boeing's headquarters.

328
00:26:02,581 --> 00:26:09,580
The Civil Aeronautics Board immediately launched an intensive investigation of the jet's flight characteristics.

329
00:26:09,581 --> 00:26:12,580
In each case, the verdict was the same.

330
00:26:12,581 --> 00:26:19,580
The pilot had miscalculated the descent, trying to fly the tri-jet by the seat of his pants.

331
00:26:19,581 --> 00:26:23,580
These findings served as a grim reminder of what all pilots have been taught.

332
00:26:23,581 --> 00:26:29,580
Jet airliners must be flown by the book, not by pilot instinct or field.

333
00:26:29,581 --> 00:26:37,560
With its reputation restored, the 727 would go on to become one of the best-selling commercial jets in history.

334
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:43,360
1,831 sales had been recorded when production ended in 1984.

335
00:26:44,660 --> 00:26:49,180
Next, size versus speed, jumbo meets Concorde.

336
00:26:50,300 --> 00:26:54,380
Dwight D. Eisenhower became the first American president to travel by jet.

337
00:26:54,381 --> 00:26:58,700
He commissioned a 707 to be the first jet-powered Air Force One.

338
00:26:59,580 --> 00:27:02,660
Commercial jets will continue on Modern Marvels.

339
00:27:05,820 --> 00:27:12,840
From the mid-60s on, the air passenger market continued to splinter and subdivide into smaller and smaller niches.

340
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:19,580
The viability of these operations are calculated by subtracting ticket sales from fuel burn and maintenance costs.

341
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:23,040
At the very heart of this equation is engine power.

342
00:27:23,041 --> 00:27:30,860
In 1965, Douglas Aircraft fielded the first American twin jet, the DC-9.

343
00:27:32,180 --> 00:27:35,720
It was a 90-seat short hauler with engines in the rear.

344
00:27:37,860 --> 00:27:42,220
Douglas had improved the tail design, giving the twin jet greater stability.

345
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:47,980
The DC-9 used two huge JT-8D Pratt & Whitney engines.

346
00:27:48,680 --> 00:27:52,240
That gave it a range of 1,923 miles

347
00:27:52,241 --> 00:27:55,660
and an economical advantage over the Trijet 727.

348
00:27:56,820 --> 00:27:58,300
Airlines were impressed.

349
00:27:59,260 --> 00:28:01,060
Orders rolled in.

350
00:28:02,820 --> 00:28:05,260
So it becomes very advantageous,

351
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:07,920
and it leads inevitably to the airplanes we're seeing today,

352
00:28:08,040 --> 00:28:12,380
the big twin-jet 757, 767, 777s,

353
00:28:12,381 --> 00:28:16,700
because if you have fewer engines, it's much more economical.

354
00:28:17,980 --> 00:28:26,360
In the 1960s, the greater use of computers began to have a significant impact on aircraft design and operation.

355
00:28:27,260 --> 00:28:34,240
Probably the most profound of these was the development of autopilot computers that could actually pilot a plane.

356
00:28:35,640 --> 00:28:43,540
Using data acquired through many sensors, flight control computers automatically adjust the aircraft's control surfaces and engine operation

357
00:28:43,541 --> 00:28:47,940
to achieve optimum altitude, speed, and fuel efficiency.

358
00:28:47,980 --> 00:28:54,860
The computer's ability to process large amounts of flight-critical data allows it to fly the

359
00:28:54,861 --> 00:28:58,940
plane with a degree of precision no human pilot can match.

360
00:28:58,941 --> 00:29:05,020
Still, in emergency situations, no computer has ever replaced the instincts and skills

361
00:29:05,021 --> 00:29:11,480
of a well-trained, experienced pilot.

362
00:29:11,481 --> 00:29:16,740
The 1960s also saw the birth of two of the most significant and controversial commercial

363
00:29:16,741 --> 00:29:18,190
aircraft ever to fly.

364
00:29:18,191 --> 00:29:22,540
One would shatter all records for speed but fail to turn a real

365
00:29:22,541 --> 00:29:23,241
profit.

366
00:29:23,242 --> 00:29:28,540
The other would set a new standard for size and bring air travel to the masses.

367
00:29:28,541 --> 00:29:38,260
In 1962, Sud Aviation of France and the British Aircraft Corporation joined forces to produce

368
00:29:38,261 --> 00:29:43,822
a commercial aircraft that would travel at over twice the speed of sound.

369
00:29:43,823 --> 00:29:44,660
Mach 2.2, a

370
00:29:44,661 --> 00:29:48,080
roughly fourteen hundred fifty miles per hour

371
00:29:48,081 --> 00:29:52,640
london would suddenly be less than three and a half hours from new york

372
00:29:52,641 --> 00:29:56,580
the radical delta body craft was named concord

373
00:29:56,581 --> 00:30:00,560
the concords power came from four rear mounted olympus engines

374
00:30:00,561 --> 00:30:04,180
total thrust was over one hundred fifty thousand pounds

375
00:30:04,181 --> 00:30:06,600
twice the output of a seven or seven

376
00:30:06,601 --> 00:30:11,540
but political bickering and multiple design changes slowed concords inception

377
00:30:11,541 --> 00:30:15,360
It would take eight years before the first prototype would be ready to fly.

378
00:30:16,580 --> 00:30:21,560
But the news of the supersonic jet would have an impact across the ocean in the U.S.

379
00:30:22,500 --> 00:30:27,460
Pan Am and Boeing had joined forces on the first wide-body jumbo jet, the 747.

380
00:30:28,300 --> 00:30:34,280
What many people did not know was that right from the start, the 747 was designed as a cargo plane.

381
00:30:34,640 --> 00:30:38,920
The wide-body spaciousness was not originally designed for passenger comfort,

382
00:30:38,921 --> 00:30:45,700
but rather to ensure that the hold could accommodate 16, two-abreast, 8x8 cargo containers.

383
00:30:46,680 --> 00:30:50,960
Later models were designed with an upswing nose door to allow straight-in loading.

384
00:30:52,600 --> 00:30:56,800
The motivation for all this cargo consciousness was the Concorde.

385
00:30:57,400 --> 00:30:59,800
In most aircraft design circles at the time,

386
00:31:00,140 --> 00:31:04,980
it was believed that soon all passenger travel would be done in the supersonic jets.

387
00:31:04,981 --> 00:31:11,300
They believed that all subsonics, including the 747, would eventually be relegated to freight duty.

388
00:31:11,980 --> 00:31:13,720
History would prove them wrong.

389
00:31:15,160 --> 00:31:20,280
The problem with supersonic transport is not in the marketing idea.

390
00:31:20,520 --> 00:31:25,940
Everybody who's flown across the Pacific Ocean wants to get there a lot faster.

391
00:31:26,320 --> 00:31:32,780
Unfortunately, the technology involved is just too expensive and there's not enough market for it.

392
00:31:34,980 --> 00:31:39,120
Fearing America would be left behind, the Boeing company, at the urging of the government,

393
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:44,760
began work on their own supersonic transports, even while the 747 was in production.

394
00:31:46,380 --> 00:31:51,620
In March of 1969, the first Concorde test flight took place in Toulouse, France.

395
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:54,680
The plane was behind schedule and over budget.

396
00:31:55,820 --> 00:32:00,540
The Concorde had hundreds of millions of dollars of R&D that were just simply written off.

397
00:32:00,920 --> 00:32:04,300
But they could never, ever begin to recover the cost, and they knew it early.

398
00:32:04,301 --> 00:32:08,260
They knew it well on they could have saved money by stopping the programs,

399
00:32:08,580 --> 00:32:10,160
but they couldn't do it because of national pride.

400
00:32:11,860 --> 00:32:15,700
Airlines began to shy away from the 118-seat payload,

401
00:32:15,940 --> 00:32:18,760
fearing ticket sales could never offset operating costs.

402
00:32:19,420 --> 00:32:22,740
The designers increased the airframe to hold 136,

403
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:25,960
but still the airlines stayed away in droves.

404
00:32:26,540 --> 00:32:29,080
The Europeans tried everything to show off their plane.

405
00:32:29,081 --> 00:32:36,080
They've had some demonstrations in which a 747 and a Concorde would take off at the same time.

406
00:32:36,760 --> 00:32:42,540
The Concorde would fly to Europe, land, load passengers, and fly back and land back at the starting part

407
00:32:42,541 --> 00:32:45,120
before the 747 landed in London.

408
00:32:45,760 --> 00:32:48,520
These demonstrations hurt rather than helped,

409
00:32:49,100 --> 00:32:54,780
proving that fuel burn for a transatlantic Concorde flight was 20% greater than that of a 707.

410
00:32:54,781 --> 00:33:00,180
Some scientists were also claiming that the high-flying plane would damage the ozone.

411
00:33:00,181 --> 00:33:03,180
Another major hurdle was noise.

412
00:33:03,181 --> 00:33:10,380
People didn't want you flying supersonically over the continent and shattering windows everywhere.

413
00:33:10,381 --> 00:33:17,980
The phenomena of sonic boom occurs when an object overtakes and collides with its own sound.

414
00:33:17,981 --> 00:33:22,580
This creates a shockwave in the atmosphere similar to a clap of thunder.

415
00:33:22,581 --> 00:33:33,440
There has been no solution found or even hinted at for eliminating sonic boom that would allow it to cross populated land masses.

416
00:33:34,100 --> 00:33:41,040
So you have to fly it over the water, it's got to go long distances, and it's going to be very expensive to fly.

417
00:33:43,300 --> 00:33:47,960
Boeing's SST made it as far as the mock-up stage before the project was killed.

418
00:33:47,961 --> 00:33:52,660
The Concorde was relegated to a high-speed charter service for the wealthy.

419
00:33:54,080 --> 00:34:01,640
Though it had been a financial fizzle, it had taught the European plane makers that they must join forces to compete with the Americans.

420
00:34:04,040 --> 00:34:07,940
Next, a glimpse into the future of commercial aviation.

421
00:34:09,600 --> 00:34:16,840
A commercial jet's cockpit voice recorder, often called the black box, is actually bright orange.

422
00:34:17,580 --> 00:34:20,620
Commercial jets will return on Modern Marvels.

423
00:34:22,760 --> 00:34:26,620
After 20-some years of dominating the commercial jet industry,

424
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:32,300
Boeing awoke in the 1970s to find its position being challenged from all sides.

425
00:34:33,140 --> 00:34:36,380
The period was called the Wide Body Wars.

426
00:34:37,320 --> 00:34:41,640
Douglas Aircraft in California and the McDonald Aircraft Corporation of St. Louis

427
00:34:41,641 --> 00:34:44,620
had merged to become the McDonald-Douglas Corporation.

428
00:34:45,560 --> 00:34:50,100
This merger had produced the DC-10, a 380-passenger jet.

429
00:34:51,100 --> 00:34:56,460
And from across the ocean came the A300 from Airbus industry.

430
00:34:58,100 --> 00:35:01,040
Airbus came into being roughly 30 years ago

431
00:35:01,041 --> 00:35:06,060
as a result of the various European manufacturers at the time

432
00:35:06,061 --> 00:35:09,240
essentially ending up competing with each other

433
00:35:09,241 --> 00:35:13,520
and none of them doing a very good job of making any money.

434
00:35:13,521 --> 00:35:19,000
They all built fine airplanes, but to go up against the giant American companies at the time,

435
00:35:19,480 --> 00:35:23,380
they felt that the only way to be fully competitive was to join forces.

436
00:35:25,700 --> 00:35:29,680
As Airbus was gaining ground, McDonnell Douglas was faltering.

437
00:35:30,280 --> 00:35:32,660
A series of accidents dogged the DC-10.

438
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:35,000
The flying public lost confidence.

439
00:35:35,700 --> 00:35:36,860
The plane was doomed.

440
00:35:37,480 --> 00:35:40,280
It would later be redesigned and called the MD-11.

441
00:35:41,160 --> 00:35:45,600
By the late 1980s, only Boeing and Airbus were serious contenders.

442
00:35:46,780 --> 00:35:52,060
Using the best and the brightest aircraft designers in Europe and state-of-the-art computer technology,

443
00:35:52,620 --> 00:35:56,100
Airbus began to grab larger and larger shares of the world market.

444
00:35:57,780 --> 00:36:08,180
Airbus has, over the past 30 years, has developed a product line that covers practically every niche required by today's airlines.

445
00:36:08,181 --> 00:36:15,800
From small airplanes, roughly 100 seat, up to four-engine airplanes, 400 seats or more.

446
00:36:17,180 --> 00:36:25,800
Airbus soon set its sights on Boeing 737 with its A330, a twin jet with a 267-seat payload.

447
00:36:26,940 --> 00:36:35,020
It followed with its four-engine A340, a 376-seat jetliner, a worthy rival to Boeing 767.

448
00:36:35,021 --> 00:36:38,940
Boeing was now in a fight to protect its market

449
00:36:38,941 --> 00:36:45,400
The American giant's first response was to stretch the 767 body

450
00:36:45,401 --> 00:36:47,360
But this approach was discarded

451
00:36:47,361 --> 00:36:51,280
In 1990, Boeing took another great risk

452
00:36:51,281 --> 00:36:53,680
And chose to design a whole new family

453
00:36:53,681 --> 00:36:56,180
It would be called 777

454
00:36:56,181 --> 00:37:00,220
It would be the most ambitious project since the 747

455
00:37:00,221 --> 00:37:04,860
The technology used would change the way planes are designed and built

456
00:37:04,861 --> 00:37:12,860
The 777 would be the largest twin jet ever built, with a payload of up to 550 seats.

457
00:37:12,861 --> 00:37:18,860
The design would take place on a worldwide digital drawing board called CATIA.

458
00:37:18,861 --> 00:37:23,860
It would comprise 7,000 workstations in 17 time zones.

459
00:37:23,861 --> 00:37:31,860
The CATIA allowed us to design on the computer and design 3D models on the computer,

460
00:37:31,861 --> 00:37:37,860
assembled the entire airplane in terms of its three-dimensional characteristics,

461
00:37:37,861 --> 00:37:44,860
all of the modules and the tubing and the wire runs and the entire airplane on the computer.

462
00:37:47,860 --> 00:37:52,860
With the CATIA workstation grid, a worldwide team of designers and fabricators

463
00:37:52,861 --> 00:37:56,860
could work on this digital three-dimensional blueprint simultaneously.

464
00:37:56,861 --> 00:38:06,580
So for the first time, anywhere, anytime, an airplane was totally designed by computer technology.

465
00:38:06,960 --> 00:38:15,180
Now this 777 was a $5 billion, 10,000 engineer, five-year program to do that.

466
00:38:16,760 --> 00:38:22,440
In order to accommodate the new production line, Fortress Boeing would have to be expanded.

467
00:38:22,441 --> 00:38:28,320
The biggest building in the world was increased by 275,000 cubic yards.

468
00:38:29,020 --> 00:38:33,320
The renovation used enough steel to build the Empire State Building twice.

469
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:40,843
To meet the 1994 delivery date, over 240 companies in the U.S.

470
00:38:40,844 --> 00:38:43,420
and 11 countries around the world

471
00:38:43,421 --> 00:38:49,320
worked simultaneously to produce the 20,000 sub-assemblies required for the 777 project.

472
00:38:52,440 --> 00:38:59,200
The 20-foot, 3-inch wide fuselage was built around a skeleton of carbon composite floor

473
00:38:59,201 --> 00:39:03,320
beams and aluminum alloy ribs called stringers.

474
00:39:03,321 --> 00:39:10,120
The outer skin was made from a new Alcoa aluminum alloy called 2XXX that has the highest fracture

475
00:39:10,121 --> 00:39:15,080
toughness of any metal now used in this application.

476
00:39:15,081 --> 00:39:20,200
The most critical technology on any twin jet is engine power and reliability.

477
00:39:20,201 --> 00:39:25,200
Since the 777 would be flying trans-oceanic flights, each of its engines had to be able

478
00:39:25,201 --> 00:39:29,680
to carry the aircraft by itself should the other engine fail.

479
00:39:29,681 --> 00:39:32,880
The engines were the most powerful jet engines ever developed.

480
00:39:32,881 --> 00:39:38,440
Pratt & Whitney, Rolls-Royce and General Electric all supplied engines for the 777.

481
00:39:38,441 --> 00:39:50,980
Previously, the highest thrust engine on our airplanes was on the 767, around 60,000 pounds of thrust.

482
00:39:51,440 --> 00:40:02,240
And we wanted to enter service on the 777 with at least 74,000 pounds of thrust and a view of growing that to 90,000 pounds of thrust.

483
00:40:02,241 --> 00:40:11,640
The wing spans almost 200 feet in length and a top surface area of 4,628 square feet.

484
00:40:11,641 --> 00:40:22,300
I think one of the major elements of this 777 is the advanced technology in its wing.

485
00:40:23,020 --> 00:40:28,840
The air fill sections of the wing are the most advanced on any commercial airplane.

486
00:40:28,841 --> 00:40:37,840
We've used our computer fluid dynamics analysis tools and a lot of wind tunnel testing to develop that wing shape.

487
00:40:39,840 --> 00:40:44,840
On June 12, 1994, the first 777 took to the air.

488
00:40:44,841 --> 00:40:49,840
A year later, it won the prestigious Collier Award for outstanding aeronautic design.

489
00:40:49,841 --> 00:40:53,840
In the minds of many, it has put Boeing back in the lead in the commercial jet field.

490
00:40:54,840 --> 00:40:55,840
But for how long?

491
00:40:55,841 --> 00:41:02,840
Certainly the 777 experience is how everybody that can afford to do it and has the capability

492
00:41:02,841 --> 00:41:06,340
to do it will do it in the future.

493
00:41:06,341 --> 00:41:07,780
What is the future?

494
00:41:07,781 --> 00:41:08,820
How much bigger?

495
00:41:08,821 --> 00:41:10,240
How much faster?

496
00:41:10,241 --> 00:41:16,060
How long will the three decade old 747 remain the queen of the skies?

497
00:41:16,061 --> 00:41:18,560
One answer will come from Airbus industry.

498
00:41:18,561 --> 00:41:26,800
In 2006, the European consortium plans to roll its A3XX, a double-decker jumbo larger than the 747.

499
00:41:28,620 --> 00:41:32,420
The 747 is older than Airbus industry.

500
00:41:33,280 --> 00:41:38,700
It's time for a new airplane to be built with state-of-the-art technology from the wheels up.

501
00:41:39,380 --> 00:41:42,140
And that's what will happen with the A3XX.

502
00:41:42,141 --> 00:41:52,480
With $10 billion in development, the A3XX will boast a 555 seat capacity with three classes of service.

503
00:41:53,140 --> 00:42:00,620
It also may provide passengers with luxuries never before seen on any airliner, such as gyms, theaters and other frills.

504
00:42:01,540 --> 00:42:06,660
Airlines will be able to, at their choice, to put in certain other amenities

505
00:42:06,661 --> 00:42:08,840
if they want to put in small lounge areas,

506
00:42:09,360 --> 00:42:15,560
if they want to use the lower deck area for bedrooms, for duty-free shopping,

507
00:42:16,280 --> 00:42:21,260
or for other facilities that they think might be appealing to their passengers.

508
00:42:21,620 --> 00:42:26,300
Will A3XX become the design standard for the next 35 years?

509
00:42:27,000 --> 00:42:29,020
Only time and technology can tell.

510
00:42:29,720 --> 00:42:35,460
One radical alternative for the future is currently being researched at the NASA Space Center at Langley, Virginia.

511
00:42:36,280 --> 00:42:42,560
This futuristic super-jumbo concept is known as the BWB, or blended-wing body.

512
00:42:43,300 --> 00:42:53,480
The designers of this delta-shaped flying wing of the future are working on ways to carry 800 passenger payloads across oceans at speeds of 560 miles per hour.

513
00:42:54,320 --> 00:43:00,660
Many experts believe that airport design will not be able to keep pace with such quantum leaps in aircraft size and capacity.

514
00:43:02,100 --> 00:43:05,700
Commercial aviation has come a long way in a very short time.

515
00:43:07,480 --> 00:43:15,900
One of the driving forces in this technological explosion has been an intense competition among a worldwide field of high-stakes players.

516
00:43:15,901 --> 00:43:21,860
But ironically, with mergers and takeovers on the rise, the future commercial jet industry

517
00:43:21,861 --> 00:43:26,080
may be a world where competition does not exist.

518
00:43:26,081 --> 00:43:30,400
Some experts predict, because of staggering development costs, that the two remaining

519
00:43:30,401 --> 00:43:36,480
giants – Boeing and Airbus – will merge into one international plane-making monolith.

520
00:43:36,481 --> 00:43:41,340
But whatever the future holds, we can look back at the past century and know that commercial

521
00:43:41,341 --> 00:43:43,860
jets have changed our world.

522
00:43:43,861 --> 00:43:51,620
They have carried billions of us to distant lands and helped foster an international exchange of ideas, trade, and culture.

523
00:43:56,180 --> 00:44:02,580
Descended from royalty, counseled by angels, chased into a strange land by murderers,

524
00:44:02,920 --> 00:44:06,680
and adored by a son who would grow up to be called the King of Kings,

525
00:44:06,681 --> 00:44:10,540
who was the mysterious spouse of Mary and father of Jesus?

526
00:44:10,541 --> 00:44:15,160
Joseph, the Silent Saint, next on the History Channel.

